<div class="ephemera-page">

  <section class="ep-hero">
    <div class="ep-kicker">Ongoing development · Staff guide</div>
    <h2>Still making things</h2>
    <p>The collection will grow, and so will the ways we organize, explore, and share it. This page tracks opportunities for future improvements.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Built to grow</h2>
    <p>The current system is intentionally simple: a shared ID sequence, a lightweight inventory, and physical browsing through binders and flat-file storage.</p>
    <p>These foundations allow the collection to develop without requiring extensive processing or technical infrastructure at the outset.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Areas for future development</h2>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <span class="ep-tag">Discovery</span>
        <h3>Better ways to search</h3>
        <p>Explore options for making selected inventory information easier for students and staff to search, browse, and navigate.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <span class="ep-tag">Digital access</span>
        <h3>Online collection highlights</h3>
        <p>Consider selected digital images, featured items, creator profiles, and thematic groupings that introduce the collection beyond the physical space.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <span class="ep-tag">Physical space</span>
        <h3>More ways to browse</h3>
        <p>Develop displays, rotating selections, and opportunities to encounter the visual and material culture of comics.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <span class="ep-tag">Community</span>
        <h3>Artist and publisher connections</h3>
        <p>Continue building relationships with artists, independent publishers, bookstores, organizations, and other contributors.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <span class="ep-tag">Teaching</span>
        <h3>Classroom connections</h3>
        <p>Identify opportunities to use the collection in courses, research activities, discussions of visual literacy, and creative projects.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <span class="ep-tag">Documentation</span>
        <h3>Improve the workflow</h3>
        <p>Refine item types, data conventions, storage procedures, and processing tools as staff gain experience.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Potential discovery enhancements</h2>
    <p>The current spreadsheet is an internal inventory. Future discovery tools could provide additional ways to explore selected materials without exposing internal processing or donor information.</p>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-step">
        <h3>Searchable item index</h3>
        <p>A public-facing search experience using selected descriptive fields, such as title, creator, type, and keywords.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-step">
        <h3>Visual browsing</h3>
        <p>A curated selection of images organized around artists, themes, publishers, formats, or events.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-step">
        <h3>Creator and publisher connections</h3>
        <p>Contextual information about the people and organizations represented in the collection.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-step">
        <h3>Teaching and research pathways</h3>
        <p>Connections to library holdings, relevant courses, research topics, and creative work.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Guiding principles for future changes</h2>
    <div class="ep-callout">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Keep processing manageable.</strong> New requirements should provide meaningful benefits without creating unnecessary staff work.</li>
        <li><strong>Preserve consistency.</strong> Changes to item types, IDs, or inventory fields should be coordinated centrally.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect provenance.</strong> Preserve donor information and any relevant permissions or restrictions.</li>
        <li><strong>Support student discovery.</strong> Prioritize improvements that help people encounter and explore the collection.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep internal and public information distinct.</strong> Public tools should use appropriately selected information.</li>
        <li><strong>Build incrementally.</strong> Test new ideas and procedures before incorporating them into routine practice.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Staff observations and suggestions</h2>
    <p>Staff who process, organize, retrieve, or help visitors explore the materials may notice opportunities to improve the collection.</p>
    <p>Useful feedback might include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Item types that are difficult to classify consistently.</li>
      <li>Keywords or subjects that students frequently request.</li>
      <li>Storage or browsing arrangements that could be improved.</li>
      <li>Repeated questions from public services staff.</li>
      <li>Potential classroom or research applications.</li>
      <li>Ideas for displays, digital features, or community connections.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Bring suggestions and recurring issues to the librarian for consideration. Changes to the shared processing system should be approved before staff implement them.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>The collection is a shared resource</h2>
    <p>The long-term goal is to create more than an organized assortment of printed objects. These materials can help students encounter the creators, communities, independent publishing networks, and visual culture that surround comics and graphic novels.</p>
    <p>Consistent processing today makes those future opportunities easier.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="ep-footer">Clark College Libraries · Comics &amp; Graphic Novels Ephemera · Development roadmap</div>
</div>
